From: enuma-fix <enuma@local>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: subsystem_restart: decouple esoc0 (X55) from its SSR restart order

On enuma (X55 modem) running the elish WiFi ROM, the X55 faults roughly
every ~13s (missing modem EFS/calibration). Stock SSR behaviour restarts
esoc0 together with its "restart order" group, which includes the wlan
subsystem; that pulls wlan down (deauth / breaks WLAN<->modem coexistence),
and a stuck wlan also breaks 通信共享 (P2P GO negotiation).

More importantly, fully suppressing esoc0 restart (a previous attempt) left
the X55 unable to recover from faults, permanently killing its QMI transport
(QMI_LOC / GNSS over QMI dies -> GPS can never acquire satellites).

This change keeps esoc0 able to restart (so the modem self-recovers and
GPS/GNSS over QMI_LOC stays alive), but removes esoc0 from its coupled
restart order so a modem restart no longer drags the wlan subsystem with it.
wlan then survives modem self-recovery, and 通信共享 keeps working.

Signed-off-by: enuma-fix <enuma@local>
---
 drivers/soc/qcom/subsystem_restart.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/qcom/subsystem_restart.c b/drivers/soc/qcom/subsystem_restart.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/subsystem_restart.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/subsystem_restart.c
@@ -1863,5 +1863,17 @@ static int subsys_register(struct subsys_desc *desc, bool is_early_init)
 		subsys->restart_order = update_restart_order(subsys);
 
+		/*
+		 * [enuma/elish cross-port fix] Decouple esoc0 (X55 modem) from its
+		 * SSR restart order so a modem self-recovery restart does NOT pull
+		 * down the wlan subsystem (which would drop WiFi / break WLAN+modem
+		 * coexistence). esoc0 still restarts on its own to recover from
+		 * faults (needed for GPS/GNSS over QMI_LOC).
+		 */
+		if (!strcmp(subsys->desc->name, "esoc0")) {
+			subsys->restart_order = NULL;
+			pr_info("enuma: decoupled esoc0 from SSR restart order\n");
+		}
+
 		if (of_property_read_u32(ofnode, "qcom,ssctl-instance-id",
 					&desc->ssctl_instance_id))
 			pr_debug("Reading instance-id for %s failed\n",
